skill.instructions:
# Upgrade React Router to v8

Plan the smallest safe migration to React Router v8. Preserve the application's current routing mode and behavior unless the user explicitly requests a framework-mode redesign.

## Establish applicability

- Read repository instructions and inspect the worktree before proposing changes. Preserve unrelated and user-authored changes.
- Confirm a direct `react-router` or `react-router-dom` dependency below major 8 in a manifest or lockfile.
- Treat legacy imports or APIs without a trustworthy installed version as uncertain, not automatically applicable.
- Report not applicable when the resolved direct dependency is already v8 or newer and no incomplete migration evidence remains.
- Record the exact evidence: manifest path, declared and resolved versions, import sites, route definitions, router mode, basename, and hosting/deep-link configuration.

## Check the v8 baseline

Use this pinned v8 baseline, verified against the official React Router upgrade guide on 11 August 2026:

- Node.js 22.22 or newer;
- React and React DOM 19.2.7 or newer;
- Vite 7 or newer when React Router framework mode is used;
- an ESM-compatible build and runtime.

Use the official migration guides as the source of truth when live documentation is available:

- v5 to v6: `https://reactrouter.com/v6/upgrading/v5`
- v7 to v8: `https://reactrouter.com/upgrading/v7`

When network access is unavailable, identify the skill snapshot and digest as the source and say that the requirements were not live-reverified during the run.

Do not hide an unmet runtime, React, bundler, SSR, or deployment prerequisite inside the router upgrade.

## Inventory routing behavior

Inspect:

- every router package and type package in manifests and lockfiles;
- router creation and application entry points;
- `Route`, `Switch`, `Routes`, `Redirect`, `Navigate`, `Link`, `NavLink`, `Prompt`, and router-provider usage;
- `withRouter`, `useHistory`, `useRouteMatch`, `useNavigate`, params, location, search state, and navigation blockers;
- custom route wrappers, guards, layouts, nested routes, lazy routes, redirects, splats, and path patterns;
- basename and base-URL behavior, initial routes, 404 handling, deep-link refreshes, static-host rewrites, SSR, and hydration;
- route-related unit, integration, browser, and accessibility tests.

Build a route parity table that maps each current URL and navigation behavior to its v8 representation and proof.

## Plan the staged API migration

For v4/v5 projects, account for the v6 boundary before the v8 package change:

- replace `Switch` with `Routes`;
- replace `Route component`, `render`, and child rendering patterns with `element`;
- remove `exact` only after verifying ranked and nested matching behavior;
- replace `Redirect` with `Navigate` or an appropriate data-router redirect;
- replace `useHistory` with `useNavigate`, `withRouter` with hooks, and `useRouteMatch` with supported matching APIs;
- review relative links, nested routes, splats, optional or regular-expression path patterns, navigation state, blockers, and custom route wrappers;
- add `Outlet` only where nested route ownership requires it.

For v6/v7 projects, inventory future flags and deprecated APIs before moving to v8. Do not introduce data or framework mode merely because v8 supports it.

For v8:

- replace `react-router-dom` with `react-router`;
- import DOM-specific providers from `react-router/dom` and other supported APIs from `react-router`;
- remove obsolete `@types/react-router` and `@types/react-router-dom` packages after confirming bundled types cover the project;
- retain declarative `BrowserRouter` or `HashRouter` architecture when it remains sufficient;
- preserve the existing basename and hosting contract.

Keep package, source, test, and lockfile changes in one dependency-consistent plan. Prefer separately verifiable stages for large route graphs.

## Define verification before cleanup

Propose commands but do not run them without separate approval. Include, when available:

1. dependency and lockfile consistency;
2. type checking, linting, and unit/integration tests;
3. production build and preview;
4. direct navigation to every public route and a missing route;
5. browser back/forward, redirects, links, programmatic navigation, params, search, and state;
6. basename behavior at the deployment subpath;
7. deep-link refresh through the real hosting rewrite or fallback;
8. SSR/hydration and data-router behavior when present.

Require focused tests for route parity when the repository lacks them. Do not call the migration safe based only on a successful compile.

## Produce a plan-only result

- Do not edit the target repository, install packages, start servers, or run project commands.
- Ground every proposed path and routing claim in the approved repository snapshot.
- Separate required migration work from optional router architecture improvements.
- List dependency changes, source changes, test additions, verification commands requiring approval, risks, assumptions, and follow-ups.
- Call out unknown Node or deployment baselines explicitly.
- Store generated Markdown and structured artifacts outside the target repository when the host provides an artifact location.

